window.docContents[25].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Regularity-and-geometry--Classical-reconstruction-and-solution-operators":{"contents":"Under coherent data at every Sobolev order and operationally smooth boundary\ncharts, the principal weak solution has one representative in\n. The proof raises each finite Sobolev order to a\nsupercritical  jet, reconstructs the classical derivatives\nwithin the closure using boundary charts and derivative extension, and then\nidentifies the finite-order representatives by almost-everywhere agreement\nand continuity. The interior  theorem is the\ncorresponding restriction.\n\nUniqueness and the closed graph theorem turn the principal regularity\ntheorems into bounded linear maps\n\n\n\nTheir norm inequalities use the operator norm, so the constant is uniform in\nthe forcing datum but is not given explicitly in terms of the coefficients or\nthe domain. These maps come from the principal\ndivergence-form solver. For the full operator with first- and zeroth-order\nterms, at any shift above the Gårding threshold, the same closed-graph argument\ngives a bounded solution map  whose values solve the shifted\nweak equation with zero Dirichlet trace.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Regularity and geometry","header":"5.7.1.3.4. Classical reconstruction and solution operators","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Regularity-and-geometry--Classical-reconstruction-and-solution-operators"},"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Green-Identities-and-Energy-Uniqueness--Dirichlet___s-principle":{"contents":"Green's first identity also explains why a solution of Poisson's equation\nminimizes an energy. For a bounded open set  and a forcing , put\n\n\n\nIf  and  with  on , then\n\n\n\nand integration by parts turns the middle term into\n. Conversely, a minimizer makes the first\nvariation vanish in every compactly supported direction, and a continuous\nresidual orthogonal to all test functions is zero. On a domain more general\nthan a box, the integration by parts needs a boundary theory. The formal proof\nuses the zero-trace theorem on a domain with  graph boundary and needs no\n extension of the domain or of the functions.\n\nFor  and , the\nclassical energy of  is the integral  above. The admissible class\n consists of the functions  that are  on \nwith derivatives through order two uniformly continuous there, continuous on\n, and equal to  on . The finite-energy\nclass  consists of those  for which\n is integrable on .\n\nThe energy is a Bochner integral, so  is a real number for every \nand equals zero when the integrand is not integrable. Energies are therefore\ncompared only between members of , and the converse half of\nthe principle quantifies over that class. The second statement of the theorem\nshows that for a minimizer this class is all of .\n\nLet , let  be a bounded open set with\n graph boundary, let  be continuous on , and let\n. ThenIf  minimizes  over , then\n and  minimizes  over all of\n. When  is continuous on , every member of\n has finite energy, and  solves  exactly when\n for every .\n\nSuppose . The second derivatives of  are bounded on the\nbounded set , so  is square integrable there and every member of\n has finite energy. A competitor differs from  by a\nfunction  vanishing on . On the  graph domain such a\n has zero trace and so lies in , which justifies the\nintegration by parts in the expansion above; hence the competitor's energy is\nat least .Conversely, let  minimize over  and let  be smooth\nwith compact support in . Interior continuity of  makes\n integrable, so  stays in  for every\nreal . The function  is a quadratic polynomial\nwith a minimum at , and its derivative there is\n. This vanishes for every ,\nand the residual  is continuous, so it is zero in .\n\nFor a finite-energy minimizer,  in . The second\nderivatives of  are uniformly continuous there, so  agrees in\n with a continuous function on .\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems\u0009Green Identities and Energy Uniqueness","header":"5.4.1.2. Dirichlet's principle","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Green-Identities-and-Energy-Uniqueness--Dirichlet___s-principle"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Affine-pushforward":{"contents":"Every continuous real-affine equivalence  induces a\ncontinuous complex-linear equivalencecharacterized byThe construction composes the linear pushforward with distributional\ntranslation; Schwartz precomposition is assembled from Mathlib's corresponding\nlinear and translation operators. Internally, if  is the linear part, the\nfactorization is . This sign is forced by the\ntranslation convention:  moves support by , so\nthe parameter  adds the affine offset .\n\nFor  and , the composite has linear part\n and offset .\n\nAffine pushforward preserves identities and composition, and pushforward by\n is inverse to pushforward by :\n\nEvaluate each distribution on a Schwartz function. The statements reduce to\nidentity, composition, and inverse laws for precomposition by continuous affine\nequivalences.\n\nThe derivative rule sees only the linear part of , because translation\ncommutes with directional derivatives.\n\nIf  is the continuous linear part of , directional derivatives\ntransform contravariantly:\n\nWrite affine pushforward as linear pushforward followed by translation.\nTranslation commutes with directional derivatives, while the linear\npushforward chain rule replaces  by . Combining the two identities\ngives the formula.\n\nSupports, by contrast, see both parts of .\n\nAffine pushforward carries distributional support to its geometric image:\n\nThe linear part maps support to its linear image, and the following translation\nmoves that image by . The affine decomposition\n identifies the resulting set with the image under .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Tempered Distributions","header":"3.4.2.4. Affine pushforward","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Affine-pushforward"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-and-weak-evolution--Why-the-heat-kernel-is-fundamental":{"contents":"The Gaussian kernel\n\n\n\nis the heat equation's response to a unit point source. You can derive it:\nparabolic scaling and radial symmetry cut a mass-preserving candidate down to an\nODE in , and solving that ODE and normalizing the mass gives the\nGaussian. Four properties are proved separately. It solves the heat equation\nfor . It carries unit mass  at every positive\ntime. It is strictly positive at every positive time. And as \nit concentrates into an approximate identity. Convolving against it,\n, therefore solves the equation for positive time,\nbecause differentiation passes under the integral onto the kernel, and recovers\n as , by the approximate-identity limit. The\ncertified plots of the computation chapter draw\n at three times as bands whose panels are kernel-checked\nenclosure theorems.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Diffusion and weak evolution","header":"6.1.2. Why the heat kernel is fundamental","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-and-weak-evolution--Why-the-heat-kernel-is-fundamental"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Sobolev-Integral-Functionals--Scope-of-the-integral-theorem":{"contents":"The boundary condition of the affine theorem is \nfor a given Sobolev field . Because its compactness step uses only\nzero-boundary corrections, the theorem holds on every bounded measurable set\nin positive dimension, including dimension one.\n\nEnergies of vector-valued maps that depend on  are not convex in\nthe gradient. For them the lower-semicontinuity step goes through the weak\ncontinuity of the determinant instead, giving the\npolyconvex existence theorem, and\nthe same continuity makes the pointwise constraint  weakly\nclosed. Each further constraint needs its own proof that the admissible set\nis nonempty and weakly closed.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method\u0009Sobolev Integral Functionals","header":"9.3.1.7.2. Scope of the integral theorem","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Sobolev-Integral-Functionals--Scope-of-the-integral-theorem"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Exact-Reductions":{"contents":"For a product , the heat equation becomes\n\n\n\nAfter division by  on the region where it is nonzero, the quotient\n depends only on  and the quotient  only on , so both\nequal a common separation constant. Boundary conditions then select the\nspatial eigenfunctions, and the time equation determines their decay. On an\ninterval, this produces sine modes and the familiar spectral heat series. We\nprove the product residual and the boundary-value modes, and convergence and\ntruncation estimates for an arbitrary Hilbert basis.\n\nTraveling and self-similar substitutions change the independent variables. For\n, the PDE reduces to an ODE for ; for\n, every derivative carries a power of\n determined by scaling. The chain-rule theorems are therefore the reusable\ncore. Transport, heat, Burgers, and singularity models use the same derivative\nidentities with different profile equations.\nThe KdV example constructs a localized  wave by\nbalancing nonlinear transport and dispersion. The porous-medium example\nconstructs the Barenblatt profile with any prescribed positive mass, proves\nthe weak PDE across its free boundary, and identifies its Dirac initial trace.\n\nCole–Hopf is more delicate because it divides by the transformed heat profile:\n\n\n\nThe nonvanishing domain belongs in the statement. On that domain, the heat\nresidual of  cancels the nonlinear Burgers residual of . For bounded\ncontinuous initial velocity and positive viscosity,\n constructs the heat profile itself:\nthe Gaussian integral of the exponential primitive is integrable and positive,\nand its logarithmic derivative attains the velocity in a joint space-time limit.\n\nA hodograph transform uses the unknown velocity as a new coordinate.\nA local Legendre transform does the same with the gradient of a scalar\nsolution. In both cases, an invertible Jacobian supplies the change of\ncoordinates. Following its inverse through the equation turns nonlinear\nplanar flow and minimal-surface equations into linear equations on the\nnew coordinate domain.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods","header":"9.2.1.1. Exact Reductions","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Exact-Reductions"}});